S34 tests (34, all files but request_state.rs): named s34_<id>_<case>; each cites its plan
Section 22 row and the Lean theorem it mirrors. Their expected values are taken from the
signed-off Lean statements and the Defs they use (Defs.parseI64, Defs.Tower.defaultPort,
Defs.Methods lists, Defs.Sse model and checks, Defs.Net definitions and checks), not from
running the code. They call the targets directly (M06/M07 pub(crate), T01-T08 and A01-A03
private: reachable only from in-module tests), so they run in the translated feature
configuration plus macros, without transport-streamable-http-client-reqwest. A test of a
feature-gated item carries the item's cfg: the M06 test
#[cfg(any(feature = "client", feature = "server"))] and the four M07 tests
#[cfg(feature = "client")], as numeric_string_value and matches_response_id (model.rs lines
321 and 329 at 7caec76); every other test calls an ungated item or lies in a module gated
already (transport/: client or server; async_rw, auth, client_side_sse, tower: their features).
So the crate's tests compile with any feature set, e.g. request-state alone (the Phase 1
evidence command). The limiter tests build every start state field by field, never through
SseEventSizeLimiter::new, which no signed-off statement covers.

R09-R12, R15 tests (12, request_state.rs, in a new mod public_api_boundaries inside mod tests,
gated by request_state.rs's own feature as the existing tests are): named after the behaviour
they pin. Their expected values are computed from the upstream source at 7caec76 (before patch
03), not from the Lean model: seal_at's expiry (line 542: now_ms.saturating_add of the TTL in
whole milliseconds clamped to i64::MAX; 0 without a TTL), the expiry check of open (line 728:
expiry != 0 && now_ms > expiry), new_with_keyring (lines 329-374), validate_key_length and
validate_config_kid (lines 755-777), with_rs1_signing and with_rs1_fallback (lines 383-442) and
the Debug impl (lines 253-276). They cover: seal_at (private; the helper of seal and seal_with,
R09/R10) with a sub-millisecond TTL, a TTL with nanoseconds, the clamp of Duration::MAX and of
i64::MAX + 1 ms and of exactly 2^64 ms, saturation near i64::MAX, negative and i64::MIN clocks,
and an expiry that lands on 0 (the token never expires and equals the token sealed without a
TTL), each checked by decoding the token's 8-byte expiry independently and by opening at the
expiry and one millisecond after it (open_at, private), also for a keyring codec that seals rs1
after with_rs1_signing; the exact InvalidKeyring messages and KeyTooShort fields of
new_with_keyring (R15) and its fault order (per entry: key id, key length, duplicate; then empty
keyring, active id validity, active id presence); the exact messages of with_rs1_signing and
with_rs1_fallback (R11, R12) on a single-key codec and for a missing kid; and their fallback
list (appended in call order, with_rs1_signing registers its own id, no duplicates, last signing
id wins) through the Debug output. open_json and open_json_with (R13, R14) get no new test. They
pass on the copy with patches 01 and 02 and with 01, 02 and 03 (patch 03 is behaviour-preserving
and changes only the bodies of seal_at and open_json_with).

