GD3 tests (5, mcp_headers.rs, at the end of mod tests, which is gated by client-side-sse and
server-side-http): named g07_<target>_<case>; they back 07-mcp-headers.patch (human decision
GD3, 2026-11-27, following the AD5 precedent) by pinning the paths it rewrites that no upstream
test reaches. Their expected values are computed from the upstream source at 7caec76
(requires_base64, is_tchar, encode_header_value, decode_header_value, BASE64_HEADER_PREFIX
`=?base64?` and BASE64_HEADER_SUFFIX `?=` of http_header.rs, BASE64_STANDARD of base64 0.23:
padded, canonical padding and zero trailing bits required), not from the Lean model. They call
only G01-G04, whose name and signature patch 07 keeps: g07_requires_base64_boundaries (G01,
M1: the empty string, a lone space or tab, an interior space (not wrapped) or tab (wrapped),
U+001F alone and inside, DEL alone and inside (`"a\u{7f}b"`), U+0080 and a non-ASCII letter,
`~` (0x7E, not wrapped), the sentinel exactly, with a payload, and with prefix and suffix
overlapping (`=?base64?=`), prefix or suffix alone, a prefix not at the start
(`"x=?base64?abc?="`, not wrapped), and a suffix followed by more text (`=?base64?=x`,
`=?base64?a?=b`: the suffix must end the value)),
g07_is_tchar_boundaries (G02, M2: each of the fifteen RFC 9110 punctuation token chars, the
quote among them (patch 07 writes it `char::from(b'\'')`), the alphanumeric bounds, the
delimiters `"(),/:;<=>?@[\]{}`, space, tab, NUL, DEL, a non-ASCII letter and U+FF07, and the
count 77 of tchars among the 128 ASCII chars), g07_decode_header_value_boundaries (G04, M4:
the prefix with no suffix after it, patch 07's inner None arm, returns the value unchanged, as
does a value without the prefix; the empty sentinel decodes to the empty string; a sentinel
over the bytes FF or C3 28 (not UTF-8), over invalid Base64 (`!!!!`, a length not a multiple
of four, missing or extra padding, a space before or after the payload `YQ==`, which is not
trimmed) or over non-zero trailing bits (`YR==`, `YWJ=`) gives None),
g07_round_trip_and_header_safe (G03 with G04, M3: for the empty string, leading or
trailing space, a tab, CR LF, an interior LF, a non-ASCII letter, four sentinel look-alikes,
a quote, and a 1200-byte ASCII string, decoding the encoded value gives the value back, the
encoded value has only bytes 0x20-0x7E, neither starts nor ends with space or tab, is a valid
http::HeaderValue, and differs from the value exactly when requires_base64 holds) and
g07_mutation_vectors (G01-G04, added on 2026-11-27 after a review mutation run; its expected
values come from upstream Rust at 7caec76 and Python's base64 module: the standard Base64
alphabet, `+` and `/`, in encoding (`é?`, `é>`, `\t~~`) and decoding (`Pz8/`, `fn5+`; the
URL-safe `Pz8_`, `fn5-` give None), sentinel placement (`=?base64?a=`, `=?x?=`, `=?base64x?=`
are not wrapped), the suffix stripped at the end only (`=?base64?YQ==?=x` and a trailing space
are returned unchanged; `=?base64?YQ==?=?=` gives None) and the prefix once
(`=?base64?=?base64?YQ==?=` gives None), a trailing NUL byte kept (`AA==`), the count 77
of tchars among all chars, and, added in a further 2026-11-27 round, the control/non-ASCII
test on every char (U+0141 `Ł`, whose low byte 0x41 is printable, U+1F600 outside the BMP,
NUL, and a 257-char value whose only non-ASCII char comes after the first 256: all wrapped),
the case-sensitive sentinel (`=?BASE64?x?=` is not wrapped and `=?BASE64?YQ==?=` decodes to
itself) and a line break inside the payload (`=?base64?YQ\n==?=` gives None)). They pass on
the copy with patches 01-06 (upstream mcp_headers.rs) and with patches 01-07; recorded run
(2026-11-27): scripts/apply-patches.sh deletes Cargo.lock in the copy, so the workspace
Cargo.lock is copied into it (or cargo generate-lockfile is run there) before cargo test
--locked -p rmcp --no-default-features --features request-state,server,client,client-side-sse,
transport-streamable-http-server,transport-streamable-http-client,auth,macros --lib, which
reports 865 passed, 0 failed on both copies. Mutation check
(re-run on 2026-11-27 after the last cases were added, scratch copy with patches 01-07, the
same cargo test command filtered to transport::common::mcp_headers, 64 tests; each mutant
alone; the failing tests are listed exactly). Kills of patch 07's rewrites: dropping the
`c == char::from(b'\'')` disjunct of is_tchar fails g07_is_tchar_boundaries and
g07_mutation_vectors; changing the inner None arm of decode_header_value to None fails
g07_decode_header_value_boundaries, g07_round_trip_and_header_safe and g07_mutation_vectors
(no upstream test); replacing prim::strip_suffix or prim::strip_prefix by Some(s) fails
those three g07 tests and the upstream round_trips_with_encode; `> 0x7F` for `> 0x7E` in
prim::has_control_or_non_ascii fails g07_requires_base64_boundaries alone; `||` for `&&` in
the sentinel test of requires_base64 fails g07_requires_base64_boundaries and
g07_mutation_vectors. Three mutants of the prim bodies that a review mutation run found
surviving every mcp_headers test are killed by cases added on 2026-11-27 (each fails exactly
one test): `< 0x1F` for `< 0x20` in prim::has_control_or_non_ascii and `s.contains(suffix)`
for `s.ends_with(suffix)` in prim::ends_with fail g07_requires_base64_boundaries (on
`"\u{1f}"` and `"=?base64?=x"`); `BASE64_STANDARD.decode(value.trim())` in prim::base64_decode
fails g07_decode_header_value_boundaries (on `"=?base64? YQ==?="`). Five further mutants that a
second review mutation run found surviving are killed by g07_mutation_vectors (each fails it
alone): URL_SAFE imported as BASE64_STANDARD in prim (encode and decode; on `é?`),
prim::strip_suffix as `s.rfind(suffix).map(|i| &s[..i])` or as `s.find(suffix).map(|i| &s[..i])`
(both on `"=?base64?YQ==?=x"`), `ends_with(value, "=")` for the suffix in requires_base64 (on
`"=?base64?a="`) and prim::base64_decode dropping trailing NUL bytes (on `AA==`). Seven further
mutants are killed by the cases of the further round (each fails g07_mutation_vectors alone): in prim::has_control_or_non_ascii, `c as u8` for
`c as u32` in the `> 0x7E` test or in both (truncating cast; on `Ł`; changing only the `< 0x20` cast is
equivalent), the `> 0x7E` test limited to the BMP (`&& (c as u32) <=
0xFFFF`; on U+1F600), `.chars().take(256).any(..)` (on the 257-char value) and NUL allowed
(`(1..0x20)` for `< 0x20`; on `"\0"`); prim::starts_with and prim::strip_prefix comparing the
prefix ASCII-case-insensitively (on `=?BASE64?x?=` and `=?BASE64?YQ==?=`); prim::base64_decode
removing CR and LF before decoding (on `=?base64?YQ\n==?=`). Ten mutants survive every test and
are equivalent: in prim::has_control_or_non_ascii, `c.is_control() || !c.is_ascii()` and
`c.is_ascii_control() || !c.is_ascii()` (the same set: below 0x20, 0x7F, above 0x7F); trim_start
before starts_with or trim_end before ends_with in prim (requires_base64 has returned true
before on any value whose first or last char is white space: space and tab are checked, every
other white space char is a control or non-ASCII char); dropping the tab from the first-byte
test, from the last-byte test, or from both, in requires_base64 (a tab is below 0x20, so
prim::has_control_or_non_ascii returns true on any value holding one); dropping the is_empty
early return of requires_base64 (the empty value has no first or last byte, no char, and does
not start with the prefix: false either way); stripping the suffix before the prefix in
decode_header_value (both orders succeed exactly when the value is the 9-byte prefix, then any
text, then the 2-byte suffix, the two not overlapping, and give the same inner part; otherwise
both return the value unchanged); and `value.trim().to_owned()` in
the else branch of encode_header_value (reached only when requires_base64 is false, so the
value has no leading or trailing space or tab and no control or non-ASCII char, and trim, which
removes Unicode White_Space chars, removes nothing).

